Add Tier 3 publishing: draft and explicitly confirm, never agent-sent

Tier 3 is different from everything built so far: a price change, a theme change
or a merge can be reverted. An email that has left the building cannot be. The
model here is therefore different too.

The boundary: the assistant can draft and revise, and there is NO tool that can
send, schedule or publish. Verified -- nothing in the registry matches
send/publish/schedule. Dispatching happens only through a publish action on the
Edge Function, triggered by a named confirmation in the admin UI.

- New migration: publisher, published_at, publish_ref, publish_error, approved_at,
  approved_by and sent_snapshot on marketing_drafts, plus a status state machine
  draft -> approved -> sending -> sent/failed and a CHECK constraint that a row
  cannot claim sent without a timestamp. The guard is in the database, not just
  in code, so a bug in the Edge Function cannot produce a false success.
- Claim-before-dispatch: the row is stamped sending before the network call, so
  two concurrent sends cannot both fire.
- A stale claim is NOT auto-retried. If dispatch did not report back it is unknown
  whether it was delivered, so the owner is told to check the destination. An
  automatic retry there would risk sending twice, which is the worse failure.
- sent_snapshot records the exact payload, because ody can still be edited
  afterwards and the point is to know what actually left.
- Toolchain: list_marketing_drafts, get_marketing_draft,
  propose_marketing_draft_edit. A sent draft cannot be revised.
- Publishers: Kit (v4 broadcasts) for newsletters, plus a generic
  PUBLISH_WEBHOOK_URL for Zapier/Make/Buffer and similar. Kit broadcasts are
  created with public:false so there is still a final confirmation inside Kit.
- Channels that cannot be automated (partner outreach, press) are refused with a
  plain explanation rather than a button that fails.
- New /admin/drafts page. Two deliberate frictions, since sending is
  irreversible: the full text is shown before any send control appears, and the
  confirmation requires typing SEND rather than clicking a button.

Also fixes a duplicate tool-registry declaration introduced while wiring this in.
